course: dictionary
lesson: 4652
title: Old words in old stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to old stories, fairy tales and the Bible, and you may not hear them at school or at home. Hither is one of them. In the story, the Crocodile says, «Come hither, Little One...» Look at the words around it: he is calling Little One closer, so hither must mean here.
explain: Old words often come in a set. Hither means to here, thither means to there, and whither means to where? Once you know the pattern, you can work out a new word. Hither has a pair in a phrase too: «hither and thither» means here and there, in many directions.
explain: Old words also change how a story sounds. They make speech sound old or grand, like a voice from a long-ago tale. When you meet one, swap in a simple word, such as here, and check that the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have understood it.
fact: Hither is an adverb, and it is old-fashioned.
fact: «Come hither» means «come here».
fact: «Hither and thither» means here and there, in many directions.
fact: Thither means to there, and whither means to where?
fact: Old words like hither make speech sound old or grand, as in old stories.
exercise: «Come hither, Little One...» What does hither mean? | Under the water | To there | * To here | Far away
exercise: What does the phrase «hither and thither» mean? | Full of joy | * Here and there, in many directions | Hidden under a bed | Very slowly and carefully
exercise: Find and copy the old word that means to there. | gingerly | hither | whither | * thither
test: The Crocodile in the story says «Come hither». What is he asking Little One to do? | Close your eyes | Go far away | * Come here | Stop and wait
test: Which old word asks the question «to where?» | * whither | thither | hither | rapture
exam: Which old word means to here? | thither | * hither | pettishly | whither
